| ==================================================================================== |
| Detection: Windows Password Spray — One source targeting many accounts               |
| Data Source: Windows Security Event Log (EventCode 4625)                             |
| Threshold: >= 5 unique accounts targeted by a single source in 10 minutes            |
| MITRE: T1110.003 — Brute Force: Password Spraying                                   |
| ==================================================================================== |
|
| Key differentiator vs. brute force:                                                  |
|   Brute force    → many failures, FEW accounts, ONE source                          |
|   Password spray → moderate failures, MANY accounts, ONE source (low-and-slow)       |
|
| Field notes (validated against Windows Server 2025 + Splunk UF 9.x):
|   - Account_Name is multi-value: ["-", "<username>"] — use mvindex(..., -1)
|   - Source IP field: Source_Network_Address (not IpAddress)
|   - Logon type field: Logon_Type (not LogonType)
|

|
| ── Step 1: Pull failed network logon events, extract username and source IP ─────────
|

index=wineventlog EventCode=4625
| eval username=mvindex(Account_Name, -1)
| eval src_ip=Source_Network_Address
| where username!="-"
    AND username!="ANONYMOUS LOGON"
    AND src_ip!="-"
    AND src_ip!="::1"
    AND src_ip!="127.0.0.1"

|
| ── Step 2: Bucket into 10-minute windows, count unique accounts per source ──────────
|

| bucket _time span=10m
| stats
    count                   as failure_count,
    dc(username)            as unique_accounts,
    values(username)        as targeted_accounts,
    dc(ComputerName)        as unique_hosts,
    values(ComputerName)    as targeted_hosts
  by _time, src_ip, Account_Domain

|
| ── Step 3: Spray logic — many unique accounts, controlled failures per account ──────
|   A true spray keeps failures-per-account low (usually 1–3) to avoid lockout         |

| where unique_accounts >= 5
| eval
    avg_attempts_per_account=round(failure_count / unique_accounts, 1),
    detection=case(
        avg_attempts_per_account <= 3,  "Password Spray (Low-and-Slow)",
        avg_attempts_per_account <= 10, "Password Spray (Moderate)",
        true(),                         "Brute Force + Spray Hybrid"
    ),
    severity=case(
        unique_accounts >= 20, "Critical",
        unique_accounts >= 10, "High",
        unique_accounts >= 5,  "Medium"
    ),
    mitre_tactic="Credential Access",
    mitre_technique="T1110.003 — Brute Force: Password Spraying",
    window_start=strftime(_time, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S UTC"),
    window_end=strftime(_time + 600, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S UTC")

|
| ── Step 4: Surface results ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
|

| table
    window_start, window_end, severity, detection,
    src_ip, Account_Domain,
    unique_accounts, failure_count, avg_attempts_per_account,
    targeted_accounts, unique_hosts, targeted_hosts,
    mitre_technique
| sort -unique_accounts


| ==================================================================================== |
| Correlate with EventCode 4624 (successful logon) after 4625 bursts —                |
|   a 4624 following a spray from the same src_ip = likely valid credential found.    |
|                                                                                      |
| Also check EventCode 4771 (Kerberos pre-auth failure) for domain spray attempts.    |
| ==================================================================================== |


| ── Bonus: Spray success correlation query ───────────────────────────────────────────
|   Run separately after a spray — confirms whether any credential hit               |

index=wineventlog EventCode=4624 Logon_Type IN (3, 10)
| eval src_ip=Source_Network_Address
| eval username=mvindex(Account_Name, -1)
| where src_ip="192.168.20.100"
| table _time, src_ip, username, ComputerName, Logon_Type
| sort -_time
